eed care and we will  always need a really strong care system  for children we actually put lifelong  loving relationships around them so that  they've got those connections for life  how do we compare in the uk to other  parts of the world well we've got a  system which um is often not very good  at making effective use of family  networks so grandparents or aunts and  uncles could play a bigger role in  children's lives people move away don't  they  people move away yes they move away so  they don't have the nuclear family that  they might have had you know 60s  certainly but what we've seen in the  review and this is through lots of  conversations with grandparents and  aunts and uncles is often really  torturous choices they need to make  between  getting financial support to become a  special  to become a carer for that child by  becoming a foster carer  but losing parental responsibility in  the child going into care or the  relative becoming a special guardian and  then not getting any financial support  and these are often grandparents who are  right on the bread line you know they've  not got a huge amount of money they are  prepared to step in and support their  relatives to grow up and the evidence  shows that that can be much better for  children's outcomes so we aren't doing  anywhere near enough on that at the  moment and what sort of age groups of  children are we talking about well we've  looked at the whole system so zero to 18  and um we've seen recently a huge  increase in the number of teenagers  coming into care it's a particular focus  in the room  one of the things we think is going on  is that harms outside of the home you  know we've built a child protection  system that's focused on abuse or  neglect inside the family home actually  we're seeing you know the growth of  things like county lines um some of the  exploitation yeah exactly some of the  exploitation in the community that's  driving up some of the need for the care  system and we've got a care system which  wasn't actually designed a lot of the  time for those teenagers and does it  break down into ethnic groups it does  we've seen real racial disparities in  the system about the amount of help that  black and mixed heritage  families get early on is much lower but  they come into the system  with concerns  later and much more rapidly and that's  new research that we're publishing today  and  is a real concern about you know how the  system understands different communities  is actually able to get alongside  families  and provide that support at the right  time good to talk to you thanks very  much indeed joining us much appreciated
 

